# Line endings, settled once, in the repository rather than in each
# developer's git config.
#
# Windows checks out CRLF by default, Linux and macOS use LF, and our
# Prettier config demands LF. Left to individual `core.autocrlf` settings,
# one contributor eventually commits CRLF, `pnpm format:check` fails in CI
# on Ubuntu, and nobody can reproduce it locally. Declaring it here means
# the repository decides, not the machine.

# Normalise every text file to LF inside the repository. Git still checks
# out whatever the platform prefers; only the committed bytes are fixed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts and git hooks MUST be LF even on Windows — a CRLF line
# ending in a shebang makes the interpreter fail with a cryptic
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory".
*.sh        text eol=lf
.husky/*    text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF; some interpreters require it.
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Binary files: never touch, never diff as text.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.pdf       binary

# Generated files — collapse them in pull request diffs so a lockfile
# change does not bury the code being reviewed.
pnpm-lock.yaml  linguist-generated=true -diff
